{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-71370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, can be found by computing $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =2(-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{ }i+2(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}{‖∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})‖} & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-71370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)"] ["math" "-j"] ["math" "i"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36346" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36346" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point  where $(x,y)=(1,1)$, can be found by computing $∇g(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\,i+\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,i+\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇g(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇g(1,1)}{‖∇g(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{i+j}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36346" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-36346" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36346" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(i-j)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}(i+j)"] ["math" "i+j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36346" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-130271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-130271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,4)}{‖∇f(1,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)}{‖\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-130271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-130271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-130271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-j"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,i+j)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-130271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}+x+y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,0) & =({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(0,0)}{‖∇f(0,0)‖} & =\\frac{2i+2j}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2i+2j}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-71381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)"] ["math" "i"] ["math" "j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x+3y-2xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(1-2y)\\text{ }i+(3-2x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1) & =(1-2(1))\\text{ }i+(3-2(\\frac{3}{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1)}{‖∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1)‖} & =\\frac{-i}{1}=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-71374" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71374" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-i"] ["math" "-j"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(-2i+i)"] ["math" "i+i"] ["math" "i-i"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71374" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-i"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71393" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)=2xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71393" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & =2xy \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2y)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(0))\\,j \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,1)=⟨-1,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1} & =\\frac{y-1}{1} \\\\ -x & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71393" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-71393" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71393" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-x+y-1=0"] ["math" "x+2y-2=0"] ["math" "x+y+1=0"] ["math" "x+y-1=0"] ["math" "x-y+1=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71393" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+y-1=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve $2xy+6{y}^{2}=x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(8,-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+6{y}^{2} & =x-2 \\\\ 2xy+6{y}^{2}-x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy+6{y}^{2}-x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(8,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(8,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(8,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-1)\\,i+(2x+12y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(8,-3) & =(2(-3)-1)\\,i+(2(8)+12(-3))\\,j \\\\  & =-7\\,i-20\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-7,-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(8,-3)=⟨-7,-20⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(8,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-8}{-7} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-20} \\\\ -20(x-8) & =-7(y+3) \\\\ -20x+7y+160+21 & =0 \\\\ -20x+7y+181 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-36347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-20x+7y+181=0"] ["math" "-20x+7y+28=0"] ["math" "-20x-7y+181=0"] ["math" "20x+7y+181=0"] ["math" "20x+7y-139=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-20x+7y+181=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve $6xy=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(10x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(10-6(1))\\,i+(2(1)-6)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,1)=⟨4,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{4} & =\\frac{y-1}{-4} \\\\ x-1 & =-y+1 \\\\ x+y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-36339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+y+2=0"] ["math" "x+y-1=0"] ["math" "x+y-2=0"] ["math" "x+y=0"] ["math" "x-y+2=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-36339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+y-2=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-130724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=2y-x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-130724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2y-x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\  & =i-j \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,2)=⟨1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{-1} \\\\ x & =2-y \\\\ x+y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-130724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-130724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-130724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x-y=-2"] ["math" "3x+2y=4"] ["math" "3x+y=2"] ["math" "x+y=2"] ["math" "x-y=-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-130724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+y=2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =2(2)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨4,0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)0-(y-0)4 & =0 \\\\ -4y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-71385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+y=2"] ["math" "x-y=2"] ["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=2"] ["math" "y=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(3,4,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(3,4,0) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-71403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j+\\frac{1}{5}\\,k"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j+k"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\,j"] ["math" "i+j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,0,1) & =2(-1)\\,i+2(0)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-2\\,i+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-71396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\,i+2\\,k"] ["math" "-2\\,i+j+2\\,k"] ["math" "-2\\,i+k"] ["math" "-2\\,i-j+2\\,k"] ["math" "2\\,i+2\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2\\,i+2\\,k"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-65168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-65168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,1,-1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,k \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+2xz)\\text{ }i+(2xy+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+(2yz+{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,1,-1) & =((1)^{2}+2(-1)(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)(1)+(-1)^{2}))\\,j+(2(1)(-1)+(-1)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i-1j-1\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-65168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-65168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-65168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k"] ["math" "3\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k"] ["math" "3\\,i-\\,j-4\\,k"] ["math" "3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k"] ["math" "\\,i-\\,j-3\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-65168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\text{ }i+2{z}^{2}y\\text{ }j+(2z{y}^{2}+2)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,-1) & =3(1)^{2}\\,i+2(-1)^{2}(-1)\\,j+(2(-1)(-1)^{2}+2)k \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-2\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-131116" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131116" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\,i+k"] ["math" "2\\,i-2\\,j-k"] ["math" "3\\,i+j+k"] ["math" "3\\,i-2\\,j"] ["math" "j-k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131116" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\,i-2\\,j"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{z}^{2}-xy}\\text{ }i+(-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}-xy}-z)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}-xy}-y)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-0)\\,j+(\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-(-2))\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-71404" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71404" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k"] ["math" "i-2\\,j+2\\,k"] ["math" "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j"] ["math" "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k"] ["math" "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71404" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $3{x}^{2}-xz=6-3xyz$ at the point $(1,0,-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-xz & =6-3xyz \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=6.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)k \\\\  & =(6x-z+3yz)\\text{ }i+3xz\\text{ }j+(-x+3xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,-3) & =(6(1)-(-3)+3(0)(-3))\\text{ }i+3(1)(-3)\\text{ }j+(-1+3(1)(0))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =9\\,i-9\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨9,-9,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y-0}{-9}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-71411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=z-3"] ["math" "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{9}=z+3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-71411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}yz=-4$ at the point $(2,-1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz)k \\\\  & =2xyz\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}z\\text{ }j+{x}^{2}y\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,1) & =2(2)(-1)(1)\\text{ }i+(2)^{2}(1)\\text{ }j+(2)^{2}(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+4\\,j-4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-4)}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-4)} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ 2-x=y+1=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-131128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2-x=y+1=1-z"] ["math" "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{4}"] ["math" "x-2=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{3}"] ["math" "x-2=y+1=1-z"] ["math" "x-2=y+1=z-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2-x=y+1=1-z"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $x={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ at the point $(3,2,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(3,2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =-i+2y\\,j-2z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(3,2,1) & =-i+2(2)\\,j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,4,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{1}=\\frac{y-2}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-131130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x-1}{4}=2-y=z-1"] ["math" "\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=z-1"] ["math" "x-1=y-2=z-1"] ["math" "x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}"] ["math" "x-3=\\frac{y-2}{2}=z-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ at the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)k \\\\  & =(2xy{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2xz)i+({x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-1)j+(2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2})k \\\\ ∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =(2(-1)(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(-1)(1))i+((-1)^{2}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-1)^{2})k \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2)i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)k \\\\  & =2\\,i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-131127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨2,-1,1⟩+t\\,⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨2,1,-1⟩+t\\,⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x=5$ at the point $(1,-1,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=5.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)k \\\\  & =(2xy+{z}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+2zx)\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(2(1)(-1)+(2)^{2})\\text{ }i+((1)^{2}+2(-1)(2))\\text{ }j+((-1)^{2}+2(2)(1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,∇f(1,-1,2)=⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e78b28277ba759ef46d54e94a69ed10c9823758956d84dc54214e13be34d80b2"} {"question" "q-131126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨-2,5,-3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨4,5,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨2,3,-5⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "q-131126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c8058233a30fe72558967168233f58c42c1f57ac4dbe1b0e1db48bb58417769"} {"question" "e-3432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/example-3432.json" "file_sha256" "9276d214ba289447259bc9f4c579325ae6057bcbda376f143e00afc10d657bae"} {"question" "e-3432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1,1)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+0+1)\\,i+(0+4y+0)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i+4y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)+1)\\,i+4(1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/example-3432.json" "file_sha256" "9276d214ba289447259bc9f4c579325ae6057bcbda376f143e00afc10d657bae"} {"question" "e-3433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/example-3433.json" "file_sha256" "bd937349c12a1626316684bc6c42d83e539ec56fc82d3980d5eb8edd0042a446"} {"question" "e-3433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/example-3433.json" "file_sha256" "bd937349c12a1626316684bc6c42d83e539ec56fc82d3980d5eb8edd0042a446"} {"question" "e-3434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2,$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/example-3434.json" "file_sha256" "3256054b4b6be0b72d84da21a22dd539fded631e92e0c9f18bce0e058484210e"} {"question" "e-3434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$ can be found by computing \n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$   \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,\\sqrt{2}) & =(4\\cdot 2-3)\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5i-2j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i  - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/example-3434.json" "file_sha256" "3256054b4b6be0b72d84da21a22dd539fded631e92e0c9f18bce0e058484210e"} {"question" "e-3435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/example-3435.json" "file_sha256" "e3ecb38cac283a256ac5f6e3a74919216241f2b7b96f5ea450b69f5a6d5519bc"} {"question" "e-3435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y-x})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{1-2})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044301/example-3435.json" "file_sha256" "e3ecb38cac283a256ac5f6e3a74919216241f2b7b96f5ea450b69f5a6d5519bc"}] "usage" {"e-3434" [] "e-3432" [] "q-36339" [] "e-3435" [] "e-3433" []} "basis" 1649 "retractions" [[17592186124887 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $6xy = 5x^{2} + y^{2}$. Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1, 1)$."] [17592186124887 :question/worked-solution "Write the curve as $F(x,y)=5x^2+y^2-6xy=0$. Then $\\nabla F=\\langle10x-6y,2y-6x\\rangle$, and $\\nabla F(1,1)=\\langle4,-4\\rangle$. The normal line passes through $(1,1)$ in this direction: $y-1=-(x-1)$, or $x+y-2=0$."] [17592186124180 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1)$."] [17592186124180 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (1,1)\n$$\n\ncan be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1)$.\n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (1,1)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y) &= f_{x}(x, y)i + f_{y}(x, y)j \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(x^{2} + 2y^{2} + x)i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(x^{2} + 2y^{2} + x)j \\\\\n&= (2x + 0 + 1)i + (0 + 4y + 0)j \\\\\n&= (2x + 1)i + 4yj \\\\\n\\nabla f(1, 1) &= (2(1) + 1)i + 4(1)j \\\\\n&= 3i + 4j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( 1 , 1 )}{\\Vert  \\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( 1 , 1 ) \\Vert} & = \\frac{3 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2} + {4}^{2}}} \\\\  & = \\frac{1}{5} ( 3 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} )\\end{array}$$"] [17592186124181 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$. Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4)$."] [17592186124181 :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=x^2/9+y^2/16-1$. A normal vector is $\\nabla F=\\langle2x/9,y/8\\rangle$, which at $(0,4)$ equals $\\langle0,1/2\\rangle$. The normal line is therefore vertical: $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle0,4\\rangle+t\\langle0,1/2\\rangle$. Its Cartesian equation is $x=0$. Parametric form avoids division by the zero horizontal component."] [17592186124182 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$."] [17592186124182 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\n$$\n\ncan be found by computing\n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$.\n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y, z) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(2x^{2} - xy - z^{2})i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(2x^{2} - xy - z^{2})j + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial z}(2x^{2} - xy - z^{2})k \\\\\n&= (4x - y)i - xj - 2zk \\\\\n\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt{2}) &= (4 \\cdot 2 - 3)i - 2j - 2\\sqrt{2}k \\\\\n&= 5i - 2j - 2\\sqrt{2}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\n$$"] [17592186124183 :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$."] [17592186124183 :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= \\ln (x^{2} - xy) \\\\\n0 &= \\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z\n$$\n\nour surface can be viewed as the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point\n\n$$\n\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)\n$$\n\ncan be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y, z) &= f_{x}(x, y, z)i + f_{y}(x, y, z)j + f_{z}(x, y, z)k \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(\\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z)i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(\\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z)j + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial z}(\\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z)k \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2x - y}{x^{2} - xy})i + (\\frac{1}{y - x})j - k \\\\\n\\nabla f(2, 1, \\ln 2) &= (\\frac{2 \\cdot 2 - 1}{2^{2} - 2 \\cdot 1})i + (\\frac{1}{1 - 2})j - k \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}i - j - k \\\\\n&= \\langle \\frac{3}{2},-1,-1\\rangle \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{x - x_{0}}{v_{x}} = \\frac{y - y_{0}}{v_{y}} = \\frac{z - z_{0}}{v_{z}}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{r}\\frac{x - 2}{( \\frac{3}{2} )} = \\frac{y - 1}{- 1} = \\frac{z - \\ln   2}{- 1} \\\\ \\frac{x - 2}{3} = \\frac{y - 1}{- 2} = \\frac{z - \\ln   2}{- 2} .\\end{array}$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122345 17592186122346 17592186122347 17592186122348 17592186122349] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122344 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122344 :answer-field/id] [17592186122344 :answer-field/key] [17592186122344 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
